, "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_14", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_15", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_16", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_12", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_05", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_17", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_18", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_26", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_22", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_24", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_20", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_27", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_21", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_19", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_25", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_28", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_29", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_30", "role": "support"}]}, "raw_final_response": "## Analysis\n\n**Group activity is roughly constant** (step_02: 198/203/148/143/187/224 events per 5k bin) \u2014 no volume shock.\n\n**Individual members \"take turns\":** each member is active in exactly one 5k bin and silent elsewhere (steps 11\u201316): 121 & 357 \u2192 bin 1; 381, 594, 728 \u2192 bin 2; 728, 785 \u2192 bin 3; 898, 1269 \u2192 bin 4; 1269, 1415, 1460 \u2192 bin 5; 1460, 1701, 1923 \u2192 bin 6. This baton-passing *looks* like turnover if you only measure per-node activity.\n\n**But the contact set never changes.** Sampling the active member in every bin returns the identical 30-node partner set {170, 172, 195, 272, 309, 324, 325, 337, 426, 460, 462, 474, 519, 593, 694, 834, 901, 922, 932, 1044, 1091, 1105, 1154, 1416, 1437, 1520, 1619, 1635, 1743, 1897}:\n- bin 1: 121 (step_05), 357 (step_17)\n- bin 2: 594 (step_18), 381 (step_26)\n- bin 3: 728 (step_22), 785 (step_24)\n- bin 4: 898 (step_20), 1269 (step_28)\n- bin 5: 1415 (step_27), 1460 (step_21, +1353), 1269 (step_29)\n- bin 6: 1701 (step_19, +235), 1923 (step_25)\n\n**Confounder controls:** the group is high-volume/top-ranked (step_03, step_04) but that popularity does not produce any partner substitution; and the direct before/after test at the strongest candidate breakpoint (node 1269 spanning bins 4\u21925, steps 28/29) gives Jaccard = 1.0 (step_30). No old set is retired and no new set appears \u2014 the only additions (1353, 235) are single stray contacts, i.e. background noise, not a cohort replacement.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_25544f86\",\n  \"decision\": \"absent\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"regime_switch\",\n  \"entities\": [121, 357, 381, 594, 728, 785, 898, 1269, 1415, 1460, 1701, 1923],\n  \"window\": [64269, 94269],\n  \"confidence\": 0.89,\n  \"narrative\": \"No abrupt change of interaction partners. The group's members activate in a staggered relay (each member concentrates its ~90 events in one 5000-unit bin), which superficially mimics turnover, but every active member in every sub-window contacts the same fixed 30-node audience {170,172,195,272,309,324,325,337,426,460,462,474,519,593,694,834,901,922,932,1044,1091,1105,1154,1416,1437,1520,1619,1635,1743,1897}. A before/after comparison with a 5000-unit half-window at the strongest candidate change point yields Jaccard = 1.0; no old contact cohort is retired and no new cohort appears. Group event volume is also flat (~140-225 per bin), so neither activity change nor the group's high popularity indicates a regime switch.\",\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_02\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_03\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_04\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_11\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_13\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_14\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_15\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_16\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_12\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_05\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_1